= Camarilla Joins the Conspiracy

In Axton, another meeting is scheduled between [+ royalist_leader +] and Goodryke in Carlile's villa in the evening, this time also joined by DuFour's nephew Symonnet, and his chief of staff Oren Marsden. Gorrick and two other important <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span> are also in attendance. This meeting has been organized by Goodryke himself, and reached the most momentous decision of his life: his most promising way to return to power and get revenge against Staal was to concede the chancellorship to [+ royalist_chancellor +].

Despite his eagerness to depose Staal, Symonnet is no <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalist</span>, and even warns his uncle of the dangers of appointing [+ royalist_chancellor +], pointing out that he rarely keeps his promises and his appointment would inevitably lead to a one-party dictatorship. 

Marsden, recognizing that the chancellor's hold on power was slipping decided that it was time to ingratiate himself with Staal's most likely successor, whether it be Goodryke or [+ royalist_chancellor +].

- @nazi_2

@nazi_2
title: The talks conclude...
on-arrival: meissner_goal = "Advocate for a Gallax chancellorship"; oskar_goal = "Advocate for a Goodryke chancellorship"; papen_goal = "Advocate for a Gallax chancellorship"; staal_strength -= 1

[+ royalist_leader +] seeks a private conversation with DuFour’s nephew, while Gorrick speaks separately with Marsden. [+ royalist_leader +]’s effort to sway the reluctant Symonnet to back his appointment as presidential chancellor fails—prompting Standerton to describe Symonnet as “the personification of stupidity.” In contrast, Gorrick, now serving as [+ royalist_leader +]’s spokesman, takes a more measured approach. Leveraging his reputation as a respected war hero and his skilled social ability, he assures Marsden that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> would demand only a single cabinet post, with the President retaining control over the crucial Foreign and Defense Ministries.

By the end of the day’s talks, consensus forms around a single point: Staal must go. An unspoken deal takes shape—Goodryke signals his willingness to accept the vice-chancellorship under [+ royalist_chancellor +], and [+ royalist_leader +], through Gorrick, makes it clear he is ready to offer major concessions in a cabinet led by him.